{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BDFP4RHFgafi3LGhgFcvum","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DYzUhCfVJp3yaLssVHteYa","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for an EMNLP 2026 ARR submission. Your focus: **Methods soundness and Experimental rigor**.\n\n## Venue Calibration (EMNLP 2026 ARR)\n- 5 = Award (top 2.5%), 4 = Conference, 3 = Findings, 2 = Resubmit, 1 = Do not resubmit\n- Dimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5)\n- Acceptance rate ~21% for main conference\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: IRT-FIT: Detecting Benchmark Contamination in Large Language Models via Item Response Theory Person-Fit Statistics\n\nThe paper proposes IRT-FIT, a framework that uses the standardized person-fit statistic ℓz* under a 4-parameter logistic (4PL) IRT model to detect benchmark contamination in LLMs using only binary (correct/incorrect) response data. Key claims:\n1. Minimal-information detection: |d|>2.0 at 15% contamination across 4 model families (Qwen, Llama, Mistral, Gemma), 8× effect size over Guttman/U3, post-RL robust (|d|=2.1 after GRPO)\n2. Detection characterization: power function R²=0.82 across 7 benchmarks, θ-fixed analysis restores monotonic dose-response, memorization fraction 33-38%\n3. Empirical findings: 57/60 model-benchmark cells flagged, 12-model case study, classical-vs-neural IRT gap (a_r=0.127)\n\n## Experimental Design (from context YAML)\nThe paper covers these experiments:\n- **Pilot** (Qwen, n=3 seeds, 0/15/50% contamination on MMLU): |d|=3.42 at 15%\n- **Full cross-family** (4 families × 6 levels × 3 benchmarks, Qwen/Llama n=10 seeds): all |d|>2.0 at 15%\n- **4PL vs 3PL ablation**: mean +20.4pp power gain, FAR controlled at 0.05\n- **Baseline comparison**: lz* 8× Guttman/U3 model-level; item-level all near-random (max AUROC 0.63)\n- **Post-RL (GRPO)**: |d|=2.10 post-RL, MIA AUROC≈0.51\n- **Power function** (7 benchmarks): R²=0.82\n- **Multi-benchmark** (GSM8K, HellaSwag): reduced but significant effects\n- **Natural experiment** (base→chat): HellaSwag/GSM8K significant\n- **V1 IRT Refit**: a_r=0.127, negative result\n- **Multiscale lzm**: cross-benchmark aggregation\n- **Domain analysis**: Gemini uniform, Doubao selective\n- **Leaderboard Audit**: ABANDONED due to discrimination failure\n\n## Key Numbers from Paper\n- Table 1: Clean Qwen lz*=8.68±1.41\n- Table 2: 15% contam |d|=3.42, 50% free-θ |d|=0.99 (θ-shift), θ-fixed restores monotonicity (ratio=3.12)\n- Table 3: Cross-family all |d|>2.0 (Qwen 3.15, Llama 2.72, Mistral 2.83, Gemma 3.05), MF 0.34-0.38\n- Table 4: Dose-response monotonic under θ-fixed (0.85→7.20)\n- Table 5: 4PL +20.4pp mean power gain\n- Table 6: lz* avg |d|=3.07 vs Guttman 0.50, U3 0.44, HT 2.95\n- Table 7: Item-level AUROC 0.528-0.630 (all near-random)\n- Table 8: Post-RL lz* |d|=2.10, MIA AUROC 0.51-0.52\n- Table 9: Post-RL mechanistic detail\n- Table 10: IRT refit a_r=0.127\n- Table 11: Power function R²=0.82\n- Table 12: Multi-benchmark |d| (GSM8K 1.62-1.85, HellaSwag 1.90-2.10)\n- Table 13: Base-to-chat natural experiment\n- Table 14: Domain-level analysis\n- Table 15: Multiscale lzm\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the IRT-FIT framework well-motivated? Is the 4PL model appropriate? Is ℓz* the right statistic? Is the θ-fixed analysis principled? Any statistical concerns?\n\n2. **Experimental rigor**: \n   - Is the controlled contamination setup valid (QLoRA SFT)?\n   - Are sample sizes adequate (n=3 for Mistral/Gemma, n=10 for Qwen/Llama)?\n   - Is the baseline comparison fair and comprehensive?\n   - Post-RL experiment: is one model family (Qwen) + one RL method (GRPO) sufficient?\n   - Are confidence intervals and statistical tests appropriate?\n   - Any concerns about p-hacking, cherry-picking, or overfitting?\n\n3. **Reproducibility**: Can this be reproduced? Are all parameters, seeds, training details specified?\n\n4. **Missing experiments or controls**: What's absent that should be there?\n\n5. **Specific weaknesses**: List concrete, actionable issues.\n\n6. **Specific strengths**: What works well methodologically/experimentally?\n\nOutput format:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n(etc.)\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n(etc.)\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n(etc.)\n\n### Missing Experiments/Controls\n\n### Preliminary Scores (before roundtable)\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall: X/5\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Calibrate to EMNLP 2026 standards with ~21% acceptance rate."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":24292,"cache_read_input_tokens":23652,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":24292},"output_tokens":19,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"44a048b4-6a0e-498e-861e-50d9d8212329","uuid":"f7f15ec3-0938-4a12-a58f-a145251f643b"}
